66
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:47,960
You put a huge amount of energy, and I'm talking about

67
00:04:47,961 --> 00:04:51,160
kind of emotional energy, not just intellectual and creative

68
00:04:51,161 --> 00:04:53,960
energy, but you get really involved in it personally.

69
00:04:53,961 --> 00:04:56,460
When you're done, you can't even conceive not winning.

70
00:04:57,420 --> 00:05:02,780
For any competition, the developer gives rival architects a set of parameters called a program.

71
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:09,720
It might include the amount of space desired, the intended usage for that space, and the budget.

72
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:17,440
It's the architect's job to take these abstract concepts in numbers and fashion them into a workable building.

73
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:22,560
Designers use every tool in their box.

74
00:05:22,561 --> 00:05:28,560
The image is always the architect that is drafting for it, which is kind of an old image.

75
00:05:28,561 --> 00:05:30,560
It's all computer now.

76
00:05:30,561 --> 00:05:38,560
In the computer we can do a model and rotate the model around, see it from any angle that you want to see it from.

77
00:05:38,561 --> 00:05:46,560
Render it or shade it so that you can see kind of the surfaces more like you would see a traditional model.

78
00:05:46,561 --> 00:05:50,560
And it doesn't have to stay in the computer.

79
00:05:50,561 --> 00:05:57,560
This machine can take this and that and turn it into this.

80
00:05:57,561 --> 00:06:03,560
It's called a 3D printer and it works similar to your inkjet at home.

81
00:06:03,561 --> 00:06:10,560
A model starts out as a heap of plaster powder, which the printer spreads into a thin layer.

82
00:06:10,561 --> 00:06:17,560
The printhead runs back and forth across the powder spraying glue instead of ink.

83
00:06:17,561 --> 00:06:20,100
it's creating a cross-section.

84
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:23,320
When it's through with one layer,

85
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:25,840
the printer drops the base a fraction of an inch,

86
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:28,840
spreads another bed of powder, and sprays more glue.

87
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:34,660
Eventually, you end up with a kind of cylinder plaster

88
00:06:34,661 --> 00:06:36,520
that you can vacuum out the excess,

89
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:40,740
and you're left in the end with a three-dimensional model

90
00:06:40,741 --> 00:06:44,040
that you can discuss with the team.

91
00:06:44,041 --> 00:06:51,040
The 3D printer quickly renders concepts, but they're just to stimulate debate.

92
00:06:51,041 --> 00:06:57,200
For the actual competition, designers will spend hundreds of hours making detailed models

93
00:06:57,201 --> 00:07:01,800
