And if you've then added some diagonal

484
00:33:52,841 --> 00:33:58,833
members to it, you can almost see the picture that it's even more rigid.

485
00:33:58,834 --> 00:34:01,080
So that's the way to resist

486
00:34:01,081 --> 00:34:02,609
the horizontal loads.

487
00:34:02,610 --> 00:34:07,560
A more radical approach introduced in the late 1960s was to move the

488
00:34:07,561 --> 00:34:10,940
bracing to the exterior of the building.

489
00:34:10,941 --> 00:34:15,080
The perimeter brace tube system most famously was

490
00:34:15,081 --> 00:34:18,840
used on the John Hancock Center in Chicago, Illinois.

491
00:34:18,841 --> 00:34:21,400
Everyone in Chicago can see these giant X's

492
00:34:21,401 --> 00:34:24,640
marching up the side of the building.

493
00:34:24,641 --> 00:34:27,320
Designed by the innovative engineer Fosler Kahn

494
00:34:27,321 --> 00:34:30,860
of Skidmore, Owings, and Merrill, the John Hancock Center

495
00:34:30,861 --> 00:34:33,460
is a departure of sorts from traditional skyscrapers.

496
00:34:36,520 --> 00:34:39,000
Called tube construction, this design

497
00:34:39,001 --> 00:34:42,800
uses exterior diagonal braces connected to columns

498
00:34:42,801 --> 00:34:44,940
to resist some of the gravity load

499
00:34:44,941 --> 00:34:51,940
and absorb all of the wind load.

500
00:34:51,941 --> 00:34:57,940
Completed in 1969, the revolutionary design opened up valuable workspace because it required

501
00:34:57,941 --> 00:35:02,720
fewer support columns.

502
00:35:02,721 --> 00:35:09,380
The most famous tube design buildings were the twin towers of the World Trade Center.

503
00:35:09,381 --> 00:35:12,380
Closely spaced columns created an exoskeleton.

504
00:35:17,760 --> 00:35:21,540
Another building that uses the tube design is the Sears Tower in Chicago,

505
00:35:21,800 --> 00:35:24,540
which works like nine tubes bundled together.

506
00:35:26,640 --> 00:35:31,760
Engineers working with architects are always balancing how tall, how wide, how many columns,

507
00:35:31,761 --> 00:35:35,520
how big a window, how fat a column, how deep a beam.

508
00:35:36,080 --> 00:35:38,600
All have to be balanced. There's no one magic answer.

509
00:35:39,380 --> 00:35:43,380
But what if Mother Nature throws you a bunch of curveballs?

510
00:35:43,381 --> 00:35:50,380
How would you construct the world's tallest building in both a seismic region and one regularly hit by typhoons?

511
00:35:54,380 --> 00:35:58,380
Builders of the new Taipei 101 face such challenges.

512
00:36:01,380 --> 00:36:07,380
It's been five months since the beginning of steel erection on the Caltrans building.

513
00:36:07,381 --> 00:36:11,380
Because Los Angeles is in an active seismic zone, the steel

514
00:36:11,381 --> 00:36:14,380
members are more beefy than those used in similar sized

515
00:36:14,381 --> 00:36:20,380
buildings in most cities. However, some regions of the world

516
00:36:20,381 --> 00:36:26,380
have even more of nature's fury to contend with. In 1999,

517
00:36:26,381 --> 00:36:31,380
construction began on Taipei 101 in Taiwan. It would become the

518
00:36:31,381 --> 00:36:35,380
world's tallest building. But their structural engineers faced

519
00:36:35,381 --> 00:36:39,380
faced huge obstacles.

520
00:36:39,381 --> 00:36:44,380
Extreme earthquake happens about every 50 years in Taipei

521
00:36:44,381 --> 00:36:46,380
and in Taiwan area.

522
00:36:46,381 --> 00:36:54,380
So the earthquake design is the most important part of all the

523
00:36:54,381 --> 00:36:56,380
structures in Taiwan.

524
00:36:56,381 --> 00:37:02,380
Taipei is also regularly pounded by very strong typhoon winds.

525
00:37:02,381 --> 00:37:09,380
A structural engineer would like to handle these two forces in different ways.

526
00:37:09,381 --> 00:37:13,380
A light and flexible building is better at withstanding earthquakes,

527
00:37:13,381 --> 00:37:18,380
while a heavy and rigid structure resists excessive wind loads better.

528
00:37:18,381 --> 00:37:22,380
In Taipei you have this situation, what do I do?

529
00:37:22,381 --> 00:37:24,380
Seismic had to be addressed first.

530
00:37:24,381 --> 00:37:28,380
So they went with a building construction system that is light.

531
00:37:28,381 --> 00:37:31,040
They use a steel frame system.

532
00:37:31,720 --> 00:37:35,940
Then they had to make it stiffer so that it didn't sway so much.

533
00:37:36,180 --> 00:37:38,680
They did that by having a braced core,

534
00:37:39,380 --> 00:37:43,520
but the building's so tall, the core alone is not enough stiffness.

535
00:37:44,240 --> 00:37:45,280
So they have outriggers.

536
00:37:45,420 --> 00:37:48,420
They have horizontal trusses that reach out every eight floors

537
00:37:48,421 --> 00:37:50,340
and grab outside columns.

538
00:37:52,020 --> 00:37:55,680
These eight super columns are large, hollow steel boxes.

539
00:37:55,681 --> 00:38:00,960
The biggest size is 8 feet by 10 feet.

540
00:38:01,880 --> 00:38:07,980
And then we fill in the column with very high-strength concrete.

541
00:38:08,820 --> 00:38:14,220
And inside the concrete, we also provided a lot of large-sized rebars.

542
00:38:14,800 --> 00:38:23,520
The steel is good for tension, but the concrete is very good to provide the compressive strength.

543
00:38:23,521 --> 00:38:31,020
In 2003, the Taipei 101 Tower captured the title of world's tallest building and a great

544
00:38:31,021 --> 00:38:32,280
deal of media attention.

545
00:38:35,320 --> 00:38:41,620
At about the same time, the Caltrans building was in the spotlight as well, but for very

546
00:38:41,621 --> 00:38:42,560
different reasons.

547
00:38:43,320 --> 00:38:48,720
Our tower crane was essentially hijacked, which made several national news venues.

548
00:38:48,721 --> 00:38:51,120
There was a distraught gentleman.

549
00:38:51,220 --> 00:38:53,720
He was a former crane operator, and evidently

550
00:38:53,820 --> 00:38:55,520
he had been drinking.

551
00:38:55,620 --> 00:38:57,520
He hopped over our construction fence,

552
00:38:57,620 --> 00:39:00,100
climbed up 220 feet of ladder, and began

553
00:39:00,200 --> 00:39:03,440
to wildly operate the crane.

554
00:39:03,540 --> 00:39:05,040
I turned on the news at 10 o'clock,

555
00:39:05,140 --> 00:39:07,200
showed the guy actually walking out on the jib

556
00:39:07,300 --> 00:39:10,200
with a bottle of something in his hand,

557
00:39:10,300 --> 00:39:13,740
you know, waving at people and drinking.

558
00:39:13,840 --> 00:39:17,020
Police feared the man might jump or have a weapon.

559
00:39:17,021 --> 00:39:18,860
The SWAT team was called in.

560
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:24,540
After seven hours of negotiations,

561
00:39:25,220 --> 00:39:27,040
the man came down and was arrested.

562
00:39:28,100 --> 00:39:28,800
I'll shove it up.

563
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:33,200
Fortunately, no one was hurt.

564
00:39:33,980 --> 00:39:35,120
And despite the interruption,

565
00:39:35,540 --> 00:39:37,600
steel erection continued right on schedule.

566
00:39:38,780 --> 00:39:41,060
The internal skeleton neared completion.

567
00:39:42,620 --> 00:39:45,240
When the last stick of iron goes into a building,

568
00:39:45,241 --> 00:39:53,460
Whether it's the Empire State Building, the Pan Am Building, or our Caltrans Building,

569
00:39:53,940 --> 00:39:55,980
it's always a reason to party.

570
00:39:58,740 --> 00:40:03,860
On June 13, 2003, ten months after excavation began,

571
00:40:04,520 --> 00:40:07,620
the Caltrans Project celebrates the topping out ceremony.

572
00:40:08,660 --> 00:40:13,020
We are so overwhelmed that we can't even express how important is this,

573
00:40:13,021 --> 00:40:17,860
but that is a big, big day for any construction site.

574
00:40:17,940 --> 00:40:19,460
This is why we usually have these parties.

575
00:40:20,880 --> 00:40:24,960
It's a big party with a barbecue and a much-deserved day off.

576
00:40:27,260 --> 00:40:31,660
The main event is the ceremonial hoisting of the final beam into position.

577
00:40:34,460 --> 00:40:37,680
Before it goes up, everyone signs the top-out beam.

578
00:40:43,020 --> 00:40:46,120
Historically, some sites have placed a tree on the top out

579
00:40:46,121 --> 00:40:50,020
beam, a custom dating back to at least 700 AD.

580
00:40:55,020 --> 00:40:58,020
No tree here, but plenty of Sharpies.

581
00:41:00,020 --> 00:41:02,020
The beam sports hundreds of John Hancocks,

582
00:41:02,021 --> 00:41:05,220
representing the diversity of the people needed to get a

583
00:41:05,221 --> 00:41:07,020
building of this size off the ground.

584
00:41:08,020 --> 00:41:10,020
Politicians to pipe fitters.

585
00:41:10,021 --> 00:41:12,020
Figureheads to foremen.

586
00:41:13,020 --> 00:41:14,460
and everyone in between.

587
00:41:17,460 --> 00:41:18,860
Now the real work has started.

588
00:41:20,660 --> 00:41:23,740
District Director for District 7, thank you, Doug.

589
00:41:26,700 --> 00:41:27,800
Gotta get this hooked up,

590
00:41:27,801 --> 00:41:30,140
because right after this speech, they want this to go.

591
00:41:33,300 --> 00:41:35,100
And with that, the tower crane

592
00:41:35,101 --> 00:41:37,140
hoists the final beam into place.

593
00:41:39,540 --> 00:41:41,080
It's been a big project.

594
00:41:41,081 --> 00:41:46,920
This is one job I'll look back on for the rest of my life and be proud that I was a part of it.

595
00:41:46,921 --> 00:41:52,500
Coming back a year down the road or two years down the road and looking at something that you had, you know,

596
00:41:52,560 --> 00:41:56,360
at a hand in, you know, in building, you know, there's a great sense of accomplishment there.

597
00:41:58,160 --> 00:42:03,080
Although there's plenty more to do before this building is ready for elevators, air conditioning, and plumbing,

598
00:42:03,600 --> 00:42:05,660
we've gotten the skeleton up.

599
00:42:06,700 --> 00:42:09,640
The iron workers can leave knowing they've done a great job.

600
00:42:11,080 --> 00:42:12,020
I love my job.

601
00:42:12,180 --> 00:42:13,280
I wouldn't want to do anything else.

602
00:42:13,960 --> 00:42:15,740
Unless I didn't have to work for no money.

603
00:42:16,580 --> 00:42:17,680
I'm proud to be an iron worker.

604
00:42:19,500 --> 00:42:22,060
Brandon and Aaron have built a dozen buildings,

605
00:42:22,500 --> 00:42:25,580
but this will be the last one they do together as partners.

606
00:42:26,580 --> 00:42:27,900
We worked together for how long?

607
00:42:28,180 --> 00:42:28,880
Two and a half years?

608
00:42:29,220 --> 00:42:30,300
Two and a half years together.

609
00:42:31,460 --> 00:42:32,180
Every day.

610
00:42:33,000 --> 00:42:34,140
Like a married couple.

611
00:42:36,080 --> 00:42:37,620
I see him more than my wife.

612
00:42:39,020 --> 00:42:40,940
Now they're going to separate jobs.

613
00:42:41,080 --> 00:42:46,480
But how are these two friends who trusted their lives to one another going to handle the split?

614
00:42:47,580 --> 00:42:49,420
Will there be a sentimental goodbye?

615
00:42:50,560 --> 00:42:51,500
Thank God we're done.

616
00:42:53,160 --> 00:42:54,020
Guess not.

